# SSH Authentication Monitoring: Security Investigation Report

**Prepared by:** Clinton Chidera
**Date:** 23 November 2026
**Environment:** Kali Linux 2026.2 (isolated VirtualBox lab)
**Classification:** Public (lab exercise, no production data)

---

## 1. Executive Summary

Remote access services such as SSH are a primary target for attackers, and a business can only defend them if it can see who is trying to log in. This investigation tested whether failed and successful SSH logins are visible on a default Kali Linux build.

**Key result:** on a default install, SSH authentication events were **not written to `/var/log/auth.log`**, so any tooling or analyst relying on that file would have seen nothing. After enabling `rsyslog`, failed logins were captured in full: username, source address, source port and timestamp. The exercise also showed that a poorly written log search can return false reassurance.

**Business impact:** a monitoring blind spot on remote access delays breach detection, and delayed detection increases the cost and scope of an incident.

## 2. Scope and Method

| Item | Detail |
|---|---|
| Systems | One Kali Linux VM (local lab only) |
| Log sources | `/var/log/auth.log`, systemd journal, `last` |
| Steps | Verified logging, installed and enabled `rsyslog`, enabled and started SSH, generated controlled failed logins, reviewed records |

```bash
sudo apt install rsyslog && sudo systemctl enable --now rsyslog
sudo systemctl enable --now ssh
sudo journalctl -u ssh -f
sudo grep -i "failed password" /var/log/auth.log | grep -v COMMAND
```

